            <ArticleTitle>[Association of thrombospondin-1 gene N700S polymorphism with coronary artery disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>421-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the association of thrombospondin-1 (TSP- 1) gene A8831G (N700S) polymorphism with coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This study was conducted with a case-control design including 178 patients with CAD (55 AMI) and 158 healthy subjects. The TSP-1 N700S polymorphism was determined by polymerase chain reaction and restriction fragment length polymorphism analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No significant difference of the AG genotype in CAD group and control group (1.7% compared with 0.6%, P=0.375) was detected. None of the homozygotes was detected for the G allele. The prevalence of the G allele was not significantly different between CAD group and controls (0.8% compared with 0.3%, P=0.376). No significant difference of the AG genotype in AMI group and control group (3.6% compared with 0.6%, P=0.104). The prevalence of G allele was not significantly different between AMI patients and controls (1.8% compared with 0.3%, P=0.364).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There are TSP-1 N700S polymorphisms in Chinese Zhejiang Han people, but the TSP-1 N700S variant shows a much lower prevalence compared with Western populations and may be not a potential risk for CAD and AMI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
